&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The ABG (Arterial Blood Gas) analysis provided shows the following values:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;pH = 7.29&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;PaCO2 = 47 mmHg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;HCO3- = 24 mEq/L&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;To interpret these values:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Assess the pH: The normal range for pH is 7.35-7.45.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The pH of 7.29 indicates acidemia (a pH below 7.35).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Assess the PaCO2: The normal range for PaCO2 is 35-45 mmHg.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The PaCO2 of 47 mmHg is elevated.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Assess the HCO3- (Bicarbonate): The normal range for HCO3- is 22-26 mEq/L.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The HCO3- of 24 mEq/L is within the normal range.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Since the pH is low, indicating acidemia, and the PaCO2 is high, suggesting that CO2 retention is contributing to the acidic pH, this points towards a respiratory cause. The HCO3- is normal, which implies that the kidneys have not yet compensated for the respiratory disturbance.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Given these values, the correct interpretation is:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;3) Respiratory acidosis&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

hn.value:
