&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The appropriate pre-operative nursing care for a baby born with exstrophy of the bladder is&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;margin: 0px; padding: 0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;to c&lt;/span&gt;over the bladder with a &lt;strong&gt;non-adherent plastic wrap&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Bladder exstrophy is a congenital anomaly where the bladder is exposed outside the abdomen. This requires careful pre-operative care to prevent infection and trauma to the exposed bladder tissue.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Covering the bladder with a non-adherent plastic wrap helps shield the bladder mucosa from irritation caused by diapers and external contaminants. It also keeps the bladder tissue moist, preventing it from drying out and reducing the risk of trauma.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;This protective measure minimizes bleeding and facilitates better conditions for surgical repair.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Place the baby in the prone position:&lt;/strong&gt; This position (lying on the stomach) is not recommended as it can put pressure on the exposed bladder and lead to further complications.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Immerse the baby in water while bathing: &lt;/strong&gt;Immersing the baby in water can increase the risk of infection and is not advised due to the risk of contamination of the exposed bladder.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Keep the bladder mucosa dry:&lt;/strong&gt; Drying out the bladder mucosa can damage the delicate tissue and increase the risk of complications such as infection and ulceration.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

