&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;These layers from the innermost to the outermost are:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mucosa&lt;/strong&gt;: The innermost layer, which is involved in secretion and absorption. It has three sub-layers: the epithelium, the lamina propria, and the muscularis mucosae.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Submucosa&lt;/strong&gt;: This layer contains blood vessels, nerves, and glands that support the mucosa.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Muscular &lt;/strong&gt;(Muscularis externa): This layer is responsible for the movements of the GI tract, including peristalsis and segmentation. It typically consists of two sub-layers: an inner circular layer and an outer longitudinal layer.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Serosa&lt;/strong&gt;: The outermost layer, which provides a protective covering. In parts of the oral cavity, pharynx, esophagus, and rectum, this layer is known as the adventitia and consists of connective tissue instead of the serous membrane.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

