&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The correct answer is: 2) Diffusion&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Diffusion: Oxygen (O2) and carbon dioxide (CO2) are exchanged across the placental barrier primarily through simple diffusion. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;This process relies on the concentration gradients of these gases, with O2 moving from the maternal blood (where its concentration is higher) to the fetal blood (where its concentration is lower), and CO2 moving in the opposite direction (from fetal blood to maternal blood).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of other options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pinocytosis&lt;/strong&gt;: This is a form of endocytosis where cells engulf extracellular fluid and its contents. This process is not typically involved in the exchange of gases like O2 and CO2.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Facilitated diffusion:&lt;/strong&gt; This process involves the use of carrier proteins to transport molecules across a membrane down their concentration gradient. While it is important for the transport of some molecules, it is not the primary mechanism for gas exchange in the placenta.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Active transport: &lt;/strong&gt;This process requires energy (usually in the form of ATP) to move substances against their concentration gradient. It is used for transporting certain ions and molecules, but not for the passive exchange of gases like O2 and CO2.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In summary, O2 and CO2 are exchanged in the placenta through the process of diffusion.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

