&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The correct answer is: 2) Hyponatremia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Hyponatremia: Water intoxication, also known as water poisoning or hyperhydration, occurs when an individual consumes an excessive amount of water in a short period. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;This dilutes the sodium (Na+) in the blood, leading to a decrease in serum sodium concentration&amp;mdash;a condition known as hyponatremia (or low blood sodium levels). The dilution of electrolytes disrupts the balance of fluids in and out of cells, potentially causing cells, especially in the brain, to swell. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;This can lead to symptoms ranging from headache and nausea to severe neurological consequences like seizures, coma, and even death in extreme cases.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of other options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Hypernatremia&lt;/strong&gt;: Hypernatremia is a condition characterized by elevated levels of sodium in the blood. It typically occurs due to dehydration, extensive water loss, or excessive sodium intake, rather than water intoxication.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Hypokalemia&lt;/strong&gt;: Hypokalemia refers to low levels of potassium (K+) in the blood. It can occur due to excessive loss of potassium through urine, vomiting, diarrhea, or inadequate intake, but it is not directly related to water intoxication.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Hyperkalemia&lt;/strong&gt;: Hyperkalemia is a condition where there is an abnormally high concentration of potassium in the blood. It usually occurs due to kidney dysfunction, excessive potassium intake, or cellular breakdown, and is not a result of water intoxication.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In summary, the major electrolyte imbalance that occurs in water intoxication is hyponatremia.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

