&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The types of delusions commonly seen in depression include:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Delusion of Nihilism:&lt;/strong&gt; This is a belief that things do not exist or that the self is dead or dying. It is characterized by extreme feelings of desolation and hopelessness.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Delusion of Poverty: &lt;/strong&gt;This is a belief that one is completely destitute or will imminently become impoverished, despite evidence to the contrary. It reflects exaggerated feelings of worthlessness and hopelessness.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Delusion of Persecution: &lt;/strong&gt;Although more commonly associated with schizophrenia and other psychotic disorders, individuals with severe depression (especially when psychotic features are present) may also experience delusions of persecution. They may believe that others are conspiring against them or have harmful intentions.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, all the types of delusions listed can be seen in cases of severe depression.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

