&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The correct answer is: 3) Repression&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In the denial stage of the Kubler-Ross model of grief, individuals often use repression as a defense mechanism. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Repression involves unconsciously blocking out distressing thoughts, memories, or feelings from one&amp;#39;s awareness. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;During denial, individuals may refuse to accept the reality of the loss or situation, thereby protecting themselves from the immediate shock or pain by keeping those thoughts and feelings out of conscious awareness.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of other options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;intellectualization&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;: This seems to be a confusion with intellectualization, which involves using reasoning and logic to avoid confronting emotional conflicts. While it is a defense mechanism, it is not typically associated with the denial stage in the Kubler-Ross model.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Suppression&lt;/strong&gt;: This involves consciously pushing distressing thoughts out of one&amp;#39;s mind. It is different from denial, which functions at an unconscious level.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Repression&lt;/strong&gt;: Correctly aligns with the denial stage as it involves unconsciously blocking out uncomfortable emotions and thoughts.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Reaction formation:&lt;/strong&gt; This involves behaving in a way that is opposite to one&amp;#39;s unacceptable thoughts or feelings. While it is a defense mechanism, it is not typically associated with the denial stage in the Kubler-Ross model.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, repression is the defense mechanism most commonly associated with the denial stage in the Kubler-Ross model of grief.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

