&lt;/ul&gt;

en.value:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The correct answer is 1) 5 - 10 seconds.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;1) 5 - 10 seconds&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;This duration is recommended because it minimizes the risk of hypoxemia (low blood oxygen levels) and other potential complications such as arrhythmias and mucosal trauma.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Short suctioning durations help to reduce the chance of depriving the patient of oxygen and causing a significant disruption to ventilation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2) 10 - 15 seconds&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;This duration can increase the risk of hypoxemia and other complications. Although some guidelines might suggest slightly longer durations in specific circumstances or for certain patients, it is generally safer to keep each suction attempt shorter to avoid these risks.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3) 15 - 20 seconds&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Suctioning for this long can significantly increase the risk of hypoxemia and potentially lead to serious complications such as bradycardia and mucosal damage. This duration is generally considered too long for safe suctioning of patients on mechanical ventilation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4) 20 - 25 seconds&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;This duration is excessively long and can pose serious risks to the patient, including severe hypoxemia, hypercapnia (elevated levels of carbon dioxide in the blood), bradycardia (slow heart rate), and trauma to the tracheal and bronchial mucosa. It is unsafe and should be avoided.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Summary&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;To ensure patient safety and minimize risks, the recommended duration for each suction attempt in patients on mechanical ventilation is 5 - 10 seconds. This helps prevent hypoxemia and reduces the likelihood of causing other complications.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

hn.value:
