&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: 3. Affected parents have a one in four chance of passing on the defective gene.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In autosomal recessive disorders, both parents must be carriers of the defective gene. For each pregnancy, there is a one in four (25%) chance that the child will inherit both defective genes (one from each parent) and thus be affected by the disorder. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;This illustrates the inheritance pattern where carriers can pass on the defective gene but may not be affected themselves.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;An affected newborn has unaffected parents.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: While it&amp;#39;s true that affected newborns can have parents who do not show symptoms of the disorder, it&amp;#39;s important to specify that these parents must be carriers of the defective gene. Simply saying &amp;quot;unaffected&amp;quot; might be misleading without this context.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;An affected newborn has one affected parent.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This statement is more characteristic of autosomal dominant disorders. For autosomal recessive disorders, an affected newborn typically has two carrier parents, not one affected parent.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Affected parents have unaffected children who are carriers.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: If both parents are affected by an autosomal recessive disorder, they would pass on the disorder to all their children. The statement incorrectly suggests that affected parents can have unaffected carrier children, which is not accurate for autosomal recessive inheritance.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The correct statement describing the inheritance pattern of autosomal recessive disorders is that affected parents have a one in four chance of passing on the defective gene. This accurately reflects the probability for each pregnancy when both parents are carriers of the autosomal recessive gene.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

