en.value:
&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: 3. Cyanosis of the feet and hands&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;At one minute after birth, it is common for full-term infants to have acrocyanosis, which is the bluish discoloration of the hands and feet due to immature blood circulation. This is a normal finding and typically resolves as the infant&amp;#39;s circulatory system adjusts to extrauterine life.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;An apical pulse of 100&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: A normal apical pulse rate for a full-term newborn is typically in the range of 120-160 beats per minute. An apical pulse of 100 is lower than expected and could indicate bradycardia, which would necessitate further evaluation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;An absence of tonus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: The absence of muscle tone (tonus) is not a normal finding in a full-term newborn. Newborns should have flexed posture and active movement. Floppiness or absence of tonus could be indicative of neurological or muscular issues and would require immediate attention.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Jaundice of the skin and sclera&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Jaundice (yellowing of the skin and sclera) is not expected at one minute of life. While newborns can develop physiological jaundice, it typically appears after the first 24 hours and peaks around the third to fifth day of life. Early jaundice may indicate a pathological condition and requires further investigation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;At one minute after birth, the presence of cyanosis of the feet and hands is expected due to immature circulation in full-term newborns. This is a normal finding that usually resolves as the baby acclimates to life outside the womb.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

